%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: typewriter.ps -- the /Typewriter font
%%Creator: pscat -- issues #211, #212 (epic #209, design docs/dev/design/TYPEWRITER.md)
%%EndComments

% A dynamic Type 3 font, /Typewriter, that types like a real machine: the
% Courier Prime outlines (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps), moved by three
% layers of variation measured from real typed pages
% (docs/dev/design/typewriter/REFERENCES.md, docs/dev/design/TYPEWRITER.md section 5):
%
%   machine  fixed quirks per key, from /Machine: an offset, a bent slug
%            (6% of keys: a vertical offset and a lean), a weight bias.
%            The same machine's `e` sits high on every page it types.
%   line     per line, from /Seed: a carriage-return offset, a bounded
%            smooth error in the line advance, a slight tilt.
%   strike   per strike, from /Seed, the cell, the key and the strike
%            index: an offset, a rotation, the rare mis-strike (1%, 4x),
%            and pressure (finger factor on the little-finger keys, 1% very
%            light strikes, punctuation struck harder).
%
% The defaults follow the measurements, which are a fraction of a point at
% body size: a page at /Wear 1 looks clean at a glance, as real typing does.
% Raise /Wear for a battered machine (1.5 is an old, hard-used one; above 2
% is for art).
%
% The ink (#212, "ribbon ink" below) is the face pressed through an inked
% fabric ribbon: the weave's voids left open in the letter, a ragged fringe
% where ink wicks into the paper, clogged counters filled on the keys the
% machine table marks, a heavy side on others, density following pressure,
% /Ribbon (wetness) and a slow drift across the page. /InkModel false gives
% #211's flat ink instead: the glyph filled in /Ink and stroked with a round
% pen whose width follows pressure x key weight x /Ribbon.
%
% Use:
%   (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run (lib/typewriter.ps) run
%   72 700 moveto << /Machine 7 /Seed 3 >> tw-setup
%   (Dear Sir,) show
%
% tw-setup starts a text block: it takes the options (any key; unknown
% keys are an error), captures the cell frame (below), and sets the font
% at /Size. tw-set changes options inside a block (a red ribbon run, the
% strike index for an overtype) without moving the frame. Options persist
% from one call to the next; /Preset, when given, first resets /Wear,
% /Ribbon and the pressure model to the preset's values.
%
% Plain use works too: `/Typewriter findfont 12.5 scalefont setfont` and
% `show`, with the defaults (machine 1, seed 1, the frame captured at load).
% The size given to scalefont plays the part of /Size.
%
% Cells. Every random value is keyed by the strike's cell: BuildChar maps
% its origin back into the frame and rounds it to a column (the glyph's own
% advance) and a line. Lines are counted in half-line steps of 1/12 inch,
% whatever the type size, as a typewriter's platen ratchets: single, 1.5
% and double spacing all land on whole steps, and any two baselines at
% least 6pt apart get different line keys. Rounding to whole cells keeps
% pscat and gs on the same draws despite sub-pixel differences. The frame is the CTM
% when tw-setup runs, moved to the current point if there is one, so an
% artist's rotate or scale before typing keeps whole lines on one grid. Text
% placed off the grid lands in its nearest cell.
%
% Purity. BuildChar is a pure function of the options, the cell, the code
% and the strike index: `stringwidth` and `charpath` run it and change
% nothing a later `show` draws. Glyphs are drawn under setcharwidth, never
% setcachedevice, so gs never caches one and replays it (TYPEWRITER.md
% section 4). The library never calls rand or srand: its draws are hashes
% through a private permutation table, so the caller's rand stream is
% untouched and `--sweep-seed` varies a piece's composition, not its typing.
% The same /Machine and /Seed give the same page, in pscat and in gs.
%
% Limits, accepted (notes/211-typewriter-font.md):
%   - It is a Type 3 font, so `charpath` gives no outline (docs/dev/FONTS.md,
%     Decision 6) and lib/lettering.ps cannot fill it. Use
%     /CourierPrime-Regular (fonts/catalog/CourierPrime) for outline work.
%   - Printable ASCII only (32-126). Any other byte advances one cell and
%     draws nothing, as a key the machine does not have. The apostrophe
%     and grave are the typewriter's straight ' and `, not the curly
%     quotes StandardEncoding names for codes 39 and 96.
%
% gs: gs -dNOSAFER (or --permit-file-read=lib/) to run the files, as for
% every library here.

% @requires: (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run

% @internal
/TWState << >> def
% The side dict is filled inside one procedure, run once, so the scan that
% builds the capability catalog (build.rs) sees one top-level definition
% here, not the side dict's own.
TWState begin {

% ---- the private hash (TYPEWRITER.md section 7) ---------------------------
% A 256-entry permutation, shuffled once from a fixed constant by the
% Schrage-form minimal-standard LCG (paintkit's pjrand). The seeds never
% shuffle it: they enter as hash keys, so a new /Seed never re-rolls the
% machine's quirks.
/tLcg 1 def
/tNext {
    tLcg 127773 idiv 2836 mul
    tLcg 127773 mod 16807 mul exch sub
    dup 0 le { 2147483647 add } if
    dup /tLcg exch def
} bind def
/P [ 0 1 255 { } for ] def
255 -1 1 {
    /tI exch def
    /tJ tNext tI 1 add mod def
    P tI get P tJ get P tI 3 -1 roll put P tJ 3 -1 roll put
} for

% a b k -> a' b' for k >= 0. Two Pearson chains over the bytes of k (the
% second with a different start and offset), so the state is 16 bits: an
% 8-bit state would give one cell in 256 the same draws as another. Each key
% is folded a byte at a time, so keys above 255 stay distinct.
/mix2u {
    {
        3 1 roll
        2 index 255 and
        dup 4 -1 roll add 101 add 255 and P exch get
        3 1 roll
        add 255 and P exch get
        exch
        3 -1 roll -8 bitshift
        dup 0 eq { pop exit } if
    } loop
} bind def
% a b k -> a' b' for any integer (or a real, truncated). A negative key
% (a cell left of or below the frame origin, a negative seed) folds as
% -(k+1), which cannot overflow even at the most negative integer, then a
% marker byte.
/mix2 {
    cvi dup 0 lt { 1 add neg mix2u 255 mix2u } { mix2u } ifelse
} bind def

% slot -> uniform in (0, 1), from the prefix state hA hB. Each quantity
% passes its own slot as a separate key: adding a slot to another key would
% make column c+1's x equal column c's y (TYPEWRITER.md section 7). The two
% chains never mix inside mix2, so the high byte hashes both of them: from
% one chain alone, two prefixes sharing hA (one pair in 256) would agree on
% the high byte of every draw.
/uni {
    hA hB 3 -1 roll mix2
    exch 1 index add 255 and P exch get 256 mul add
    0.5 add 65536 div
} bind def
% slot -> roughly normal, sd 1 (three uniforms)
/gau {
    4 mul dup 1 add uni exch dup 2 add uni exch 3 add uni
    add add 1.5 sub 2 mul
} bind def
% prefix states for the three domains, so a machine and a document with
% the same number never share draws
/tDomain { 0 1 3 -1 roll mix2 3 -1 roll mix2 } bind def   % n k -> a b

% ---- defaults -------------------------------------------------------------
/tMachine 1 def  /tSeed 1 def  /tStrike 0 def
/tWear 1 def  /tMWear 1 def  /tLWear 1 def  /tSWear 1 def
/tPitch 10 def  /tSize 12.5 def
/tInk [0.13 0.13 0.15] def  /tRibbon 0.7 def
/tCopies 0 def  /tCopyC 0 def
/tPSd 0.12 def  /tFinger true def  /tInkOn true def
/tMat matrix def
/tFillAdj /.setfilladjust2 where { pop true } { false } ifelse def
/tMtabFor null def  /tDocFor null def  /tLineFor null def

% /Preset -> /Wear, /Ribbon and the pressure model (TYPEWRITER.md section 5)
/tPresets <<
    /manual   << /tWear 1   /tRibbon 0.7  /tPSd 0.12 /tFinger true >>
    /electric << /tWear 0.7 /tRibbon 0.7  /tPSd 0.03 /tFinger false >>
    /worn     << /tWear 1.5 /tRibbon 0.35 /tPSd 0.12 /tFinger true >>
>> def

% pressure adjustments by code: the little-finger keys (q a z p ; / - and
% their shifts) strike lighter; . , ' " concentrate the blow on a small face
% and print darker (TYPEWRITER.md Q3: embossing as extra pressure)
/tFingerAdj 128 array def
0 1 127 { tFingerAdj exch 0 put } for
(qazp;/-QAZP:?_) { tFingerAdj exch -0.1 put } forall
/tEmbossAdj 128 array def
0 1 127 { tEmbossAdj exch 0 put } for
(.,'") { tEmbossAdj exch 0.15 put } forall

% ---- the machine table ----------------------------------------------------
% One entry per printable code, at /Wear 1 (wear scales them at the strike):
% [ x y rotation weight-deviation clog-fraction one-side-angle one-side-strength ]
% in glyph units (1000/em; 80 units = 1pt at /Size 12.5) and degrees.
% clog and one-side are 0 for keys without the quirk; #212 draws them.
/tMbuild {
    /tMtab 95 array def
    32 1 126 {
        /kCode exch def
        tMA tMB kCode mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
        1 gau 6.4 mul                               % x: sd 0.08pt
        2 gau 6.4 mul                               % y: sd 0.08pt
        0                                           % lean
        3 uni 0.06 lt {                             % bent slug, 6% of keys
            pop
            4 uni 12 mul 8 add 5 uni 0.5 lt { neg } if add   % 0.1-0.25pt
            6 uni 0.5 mul 0.3 add 7 uni 0.5 lt { neg } if    % 0.3-0.8 degrees
        } if
        8 gau 0.06 mul                              % weight: sd 0.06
        TWOutlines kCode get 1 get length 0 gt {    % clogged counter
            9 uni kCode 97 eq kCode 101 eq or kCode 111 eq or { 0.3 } { 0.15 } ifelse lt
            { 10 uni 0.5 mul 0.5 add } { 0 } ifelse
        } { 0 } ifelse
        11 uni 0.08 lt {                            % one side heavy, 8% of keys
            12 uni 360 mul 13 uni 0.3 mul 0.3 add
        } { 0 0 } ifelse
        7 array astore tMtab kCode 32 sub 3 -1 roll put
    } for
    /tMtabFor tMachine def
} bind def

% ---- options --------------------------------------------------------------
/tIsNum { type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or } bind def
/tIsInt { type /integertype eq } bind def
/tIsNonNeg { dup tIsNum { 0 ge } { pop false } ifelse } bind def
/tIsPos { dup tIsNum { 0 gt } { pop false } ifelse } bind def
/tIsUnit { dup tIsNum { dup 0 ge exch 1 le and } { pop false } ifelse } bind def
/tIsRgb {
    dup type /arraytype eq {
        dup length 3 eq { true exch { tIsUnit and } forall } { pop false } ifelse
    } { pop false } ifelse
} bind def
/tIsBool { type /booleantype eq } bind def
/tIsPreset { dup type /nametype eq { tPresets exch known } { pop false } ifelse } bind def

% the option keys: internal name, check, guard
/tKeys <<
    /Preset      [ null         /tIsPreset /tw-preset-must-be-manual-electric-or-worn ]
    /Machine     [ /tMachine    /tIsInt    /tw-machine-must-be-an-integer ]
    /Seed        [ /tSeed       /tIsInt    /tw-seed-must-be-an-integer ]
    /Strike      [ /tStrike     /tIsInt    /tw-strike-must-be-an-integer ]
    /Wear        [ /tWear       /tIsNonNeg /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /MachineWear [ /tMWear      /tIsNonNeg /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /LineWear    [ /tLWear      /tIsNonNeg /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /StrikeWear  [ /tSWear      /tIsNonNeg /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /Pitch       [ /tPitch      /tIsPos    /tw-pitch-must-be-a-positive-number ]
    /Size        [ /tSize       /tIsPos    /tw-size-must-be-a-positive-number ]
    /Ink         [ /tInk        /tIsRgb    /tw-ink-must-be-an-rgb-array ]
    /Ribbon      [ /tRibbon     /tIsUnit   /tw-ribbon-must-be-0-to-1 ]
    /InkModel    [ /tInkOn      /tIsBool   /tw-inkmodel-must-be-a-boolean ]
    /Copies      [ /tCopies     /tIsNonNeg /tw-copies-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
>> def

% opts -> -  (TWState is the current dict, begun by the caller). Every key
% is checked before any is applied, so a rejected call changes nothing, and
% each guard ends TWState first, so a caller's `stopped` does not leave it
% on the dict stack to catch the caller's next def.
/tApply {
    dup type /dicttype ne { pop end tw-options-must-be-a-dict } if
    dup { pop tKeys exch known not { end tw-unknown-option } if } forall
    dup {
        exch tKeys exch get aload pop               % value internal check guard
        3 index 2 index cvx exec { pop pop pop pop } { 4 1 roll pop pop pop end cvx exec } ifelse
    } forall
    dup /Preset known { dup /Preset get tPresets exch get { def } forall } if
    {
        exch tKeys exch get 0 get dup null eq { pop pop } { exch def } ifelse
    } forall
    tDerive
} bind def

% the values every strike reads, from the options
/tDerive {
    /advU 72000 tPitch div tSize div def        % the advance, glyph units
    /xS advU 600 div def                        % the face is drawn on 600
    /wearM tWear tMWear mul def
    /wearL tWear tLWear mul def
    /wearS tWear tSWear mul def
    % photocopy generations (#214): 0 at no copies, so no copy branch runs
    /tCopyC 1 0.55 tCopies exp sub def
    % the line memo holds values scaled by the wear: any option change
    % drops it, so a strike never depends on what was typed before
    /tLineFor null def
    tDocFor tSeed ne {
        5 tSeed tDomain /docB exch def /docA exch def
        /tDocFor tSeed def
    } if
    tMtabFor tMachine ne {
        6 tMachine tDomain /tMB exch def /tMA exch def
        tMbuild
    } if
} bind def

% the cell frame: the CTM, moved to the current point if there is one
/tFrame {
    { currentpoint } stopped { matrix } { matrix translate } ifelse
    matrix currentmatrix matrix concatmatrix matrix invertmatrix
    /frameInv exch def
    /tLineFor null def
} bind def

% ---- the strike -----------------------------------------------------------
/tLen { dup mul exch dup mul add sqrt } bind def

% knot -> -1..1, the line-advance noise at a knot
/tKnot {
    docA docB 3 mix2 3 -1 roll mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
    1 uni 2 mul 1 sub
} bind def

% the line layer, for kLine (glyph units and degrees)
/tLbuild {
    docA docB 1 mix2 kLine mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
    /lX 1 gau 16 mul wearL mul def              % carriage return: sd 0.2pt
    /lTilt 2 gau 0.08 mul wearL mul def         % tilt: sd 0.08 degrees
    % advance error: smooth in the line index, bounded at 0.3pt (not a
    % random walk), knots every two lines (four half-line steps),
    % smoothstep between them
    kLine 4 div dup floor dup /tKn exch def sub
    dup dup mul exch 2 mul 3 exch sub mul /tS exch def
    tKn tKnot 1 tS sub mul tKn 1 add tKnot tS mul add 24 mul wearL mul /lY exch def
    /tLineFor kLine def
} bind def

% code -> -  draws one strike in the current cell (TWState is current)
/tStrikeGlyph {
    /kCode exch def
    % the cell: the glyph origin in the frame, in advances and half-lines.
    % The column pitch is the advance as drawn, so plain `12 scalefont` use
    % (a 6.9pt advance) still gives each glyph its own column. The line
    % pitch is a fixed 6pt of the frame, never derived from /Size, which
    % plain use leaves at its default.
    0 0 transform frameInv transform /fY exch def /fX exch def
    advU 0 dtransform frameInv dtransform tLen /cellW exch def
    fX cellW div round cvi /kCol exch def
    fY 6 div round cvi /kLine exch def

    tLineFor kLine ne { tLbuild } if
    tMtab kCode 32 sub get aload pop
    /mSS exch def /mSA exch def /mClog exch def /mWt exch def /mR exch def /mY exch def /mX exch def

    docA docB 2 mix2 kLine mix2 kCol mix2 kCode mix2 tStrike mix2
    /hB exch def /hA exch def
    /sX 1 gau 16 mul wearS mul def              % sd 0.2pt
    /sY 2 gau 12 mul wearS mul def              % sd 0.15pt
    /sR 3 gau 0.25 mul wearS mul def            % sd 0.25 degrees
    4 uni 0.01 lt { /sX sX 4 mul def /sY sY 4 mul def } if    % mis-strike
    % pressure: a deviation from 1, scaled by the strike wear
    5 uni tPSd 6 div lt { 8 uni 0.4 mul 0.3 add neg } {   % very light: -0.3 to -0.7, 2% at sd 0.12
        6 gau tPSd mul
        tFinger { tFingerAdj kCode get add } if
        tEmbossAdj kCode get add
    } ifelse
    wearS mul 1 add dup 0.1 lt { pop 0.1 } if
    mWt wearM mul 1 add dup 0.1 lt { pop 0.1 } if mul
    /tPr exch def
    tPr 48 mul tRibbon mul
    tCopyC 0 gt { 1 tCopyC 1.1 mul add mul } if     % copies spread the toner
    /inkW exch def                                  % spread: 0.6pt x ribbon at 1

    % place: line + machine + strike offsets, the tilt's rise along the
    % line, then the rotations about the middle of the face
    lX mX wearM mul add sX add
    lY mY wearM mul add sY add kCol advU mul lTilt sin mul lTilt cos div add
    translate
    advU 2 div 250 translate
    lTilt mR wearM mul add sR add rotate
    advU 2 div neg -250 translate

    tInk aload pop setrgbcolor
    % gs dilates every fill and stroke by part of a pixel, which at body
    % size closes the counters of elite type; the stroke here already is
    % the ink's spread (DRYMEDIA.md 2.4; TYPEWRITER.md section 6). pscat
    % has no fill adjust and skips this.
    tFillAdj { 0 0 .setfilladjust2 } if
    tInkOn { tInkStrike } {
    TWOutlines kCode get
    tMat currentmatrix pop
    xS 1 scale newpath dup 0 get exec 1 get exec
    tMat setmatrix                              % a round pen, not a squeezed one
    inkW 0 gt {
        % a solid pen with round joins whatever the caller left in the
        % graphics state: a dash would break the ink, and a round or square
        % cap marks the outline's zero-length closing segments
        gsave fill grestore
        inkW setlinewidth 1 setlinejoin 0 setlinecap [] 0 setdash stroke
    } { fill } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% ---- ribbon ink (#212; TYPEWRITER.md section 6) ----------------------------
% The letter is the type face pressed through an inked fabric ribbon. Each
% strike draws its ink through a clip that leaves the weave's voids open
% (holes are a clip, never paper-coloured paint, so whatever is under the
% page shows through them), in up to four passes:
%
%   feather  the outline stroked wider than the spread, through a sparse
%            weave: the ragged fringe of ink wicking into the paper
%   body     the glyph filled and stroked with the pressure-driven spread,
%            through the strike's weave; a one-side-heavy key draws it
%            through a sparser weave, then again through the normal one on
%            its heavy half only
%            (a light strike's body is also clipped to the glyph shifted four
%            ways, so its strokes thin: #477)
%   counter  a clogged key's counters filled through the weave, over the
%            fraction of each counter the dirt has packed
%
% The weave is a bank of patches, 8 x 8 yarns at 0.48pt (150 yarns to the
% inch: the ribbon does not scale with the type), built once at load from
% the fixed hash: 4 variants, each at 16 hole levels (hole probability 0,
% 0.05 ... 0.75). Each yarn cell has one jittered quadrilateral hole, drawn
% when its rank is below the level, larger at higher levels, so a denser
% strike's holes are a subset of a lighter one's. A strike lays the patches
% over its glyph at its own angle and sub-yarn offset, a hashed variant per
% patch, and clips with eoclip (the patches' outer rectangle plus the holes,
% which never overlap).
%
% Density: the hole level rises as the ribbon dries (/Ribbon falls) and as
% the strike's pressure falls, and drifts slowly across the page (ribbon
% wear, keyed by /Seed). Every draw here is a hash: per strike when strike
% variation is on, per machine key when /StrikeWear is 0 (so a quirks-only
% page repeats a key exactly, ink and all). /InkModel false draws #211's
% flat ink instead.

% light strikes (#477): the hole level rises by tLightSlope per unit of
% pressure shortfall up to tLightKnee, then by tLightTail; the body is eroded by
% tELo glyph units per unit of shortfall past tEDead, to at most tECap; a
% one-side-heavy key's light side is eroded tESide more per unit of its
% lightness; the feather's width falls by up to tFringe of itself at tECap
/tLightSlope 0.6 def  /tLightKnee 0.15 def  /tLightTail 0.1 def
/tEDead 0.08 def  /tELo 60 def  /tECap 20 def
/tESide 16 def  /tFringe 0.6 def
/tE 0 def  /tE0 0 def                           % the strike's erosion, and a saved copy
/tYarn 0.48 def                                 % yarn pitch, points
/tPatch tYarn 8 mul def                         % patch side, points
/tWeave [ 0 1 3 {
    /tV exch def
    % per cell: rank, centre x y, size factor, 8 corner jitters
    /tCells [ 0 1 63 {
        /tC exch def
        % the clump: a field over 2 x 2 cells (about 1pt), so the voids
        % gather into the blotches a scan shows, not an even pin-prick grid
        9 tV tDomain tC 8 mod 2 idiv mix2 tC 8 idiv 2 idiv mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
        1 uni
        8 tV tDomain tC mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
        [
            % the mean of the two uniforms, mapped back to uniform (its
            % triangular CDF), so a level's hole fraction is its level
            exch 1 uni add 2 div
            dup 0.5 lt { dup mul 2 mul } { 1 exch sub dup mul 2 mul 1 exch sub } ifelse
            tC 8 mod 0.5 add tYarn mul 2 uni 0.5 sub 0.08 mul tYarn mul add
            tC 8 idiv 0.5 add tYarn mul 3 uni 0.5 sub 0.08 mul tYarn mul add
            4 uni 0.4 mul 0.6 add
            5 1 12 { uni 0.5 sub 0.12 mul tYarn mul } for
        ]
    } for ] def
    [ 0 1 15 {
        /tPk exch 0.05 mul def
        % half-size at a size factor of 1: 0.15-0.4 of the pitch, so the
        % hole stays inside its cell (centre jitter 0.04, corners 0.06)
        /tH tPk 0.33 mul 0.15 add tYarn mul def
        [ tCells {
            aload pop
            /tJ8 exch def /tJ7 exch def /tJ6 exch def /tJ5 exch def
            /tJ4 exch def /tJ3 exch def /tJ2 exch def /tJ1 exch def
            tH mul /tHs exch def /tCy exch def /tCx exch def
            tPk lt {
                tCx tHs sub tJ1 add tCy tHs sub tJ2 add /moveto load
                tCx tHs add tJ3 add tCy tHs sub tJ4 add /lineto load
                tCx tHs add tJ5 add tCy tHs add tJ6 add /lineto load
                tCx tHs sub tJ7 add tCy tHs add tJ8 add /lineto load
                /closepath load
            } if
        } forall ] cvx
    } for ]
} for ] def

% per code: the outline's and the counters' bounding boxes, glyph units
% (unsqueezed), for laying patches and sizing a clogged counter's fill
/tGBox 95 array def  /tCBox 95 array def
gsave
33 1 126 {
    /tC exch def
    newpath TWOutlines tC get 0 get exec pathbbox 4 array astore tGBox tC 32 sub 3 -1 roll put
    TWOutlines tC get 1 get dup length 0 gt {
        newpath exec pathbbox 4 array astore
    } { pop [0 0 0 0] } ifelse
    tCBox tC 32 sub 3 -1 roll put
} for
grestore

/tClipM matrix def
/tSmooth { dup dup mul exch 2 mul 3 exch sub mul } bind def    % smoothstep
% kx ky -> -1..1, the ribbon-wear noise at a knot
/tWearKnot {
    docA docB 4 mix2 3 -1 roll mix2 3 -1 roll mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
    1 uni 2 mul 1 sub
} bind def
% -> -1..1, smooth across the page: knots every 8 columns and 8 half-lines
/tPageWear {
    kCol 8 div dup floor dup /tWx exch def sub tSmooth /tFx exch def
    kLine 8 div dup floor dup /tWy exch def sub tSmooth /tFy exch def
    tWx tWy tWearKnot 1 tFx sub mul tWx 1 add tWy tWearKnot tFx mul add 1 tFy sub mul
    tWx tWy 1 add tWearKnot 1 tFx sub mul tWx 1 add tWy 1 add tWearKnot tFx mul add tFy mul
    add
} bind def

% p -> level 0..15
/tLevel { 20 mul round cvi dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if dup 15 gt { pop 15 } if } bind def

% level -> -  clips to the weave at that level (the CTM is tMat before and
% after). Level 0 has no holes and clips nothing.
/tWeaveClip {
    dup 0 gt {
        /wL exch def
        tClipM setmatrix
        newpath
        wI0 tPatch mul wJ0 tPatch mul moveto
        wI1 1 add tPatch mul wJ0 tPatch mul lineto
        wI1 1 add tPatch mul wJ1 1 add tPatch mul lineto
        wI0 tPatch mul wJ1 1 add tPatch mul lineto closepath
        wI0 1 wI1 {
            /wI exch def
            wJ0 1 wJ1 {
                /wJ exch def
                tClipM setmatrix wI tPatch mul wJ tPatch mul translate
                tWeave wA wB wI mix2 wJ mix2 pop 3 and get wL get exec
            } for
        } for
        eoclip newpath tMat setmatrix
    } { pop } ifelse
} bind def

% the glyph's path, squeezed to the pitch; the CTM is tMat before and after
/tGlyphPath {
    newpath xS 1 scale tGlyph 0 get exec tGlyph 1 get exec tMat setmatrix
} bind def
/tPen { setlinewidth 1 setlinejoin 0 setlinecap [] 0 setdash } bind def
% erodes the clip by tE (glyph units, squeezed frame): the glyph shifted
% four ways, intersected. A clip only, so a light strike stays darken-only
% (#477). Needs the CTM at tMat; leaves it there.
/tShiftClip {
    tMat setmatrix translate tGlyphPath clip newpath tMat setmatrix
} bind def
/tErode {
    tE tE tShiftClip  tE neg tE tShiftClip
    tE tE neg tShiftClip  tE neg tE neg tShiftClip
} bind def
/tBody {
    tE 0 gt { tErode } if
    tGlyphPath
    wBodyW 0 gt { gsave fill grestore wBodyW tPen stroke } { fill } ifelse
} bind def

% fraction p -> -  fills the clogged counters through the weave at p, over
% the band from the dirt's side to the fraction of the counter's circle
/tClogBand {
    exch dup 0 gt {
        gsave
            exch tLevel tWeaveClip
            tCx0 tCx1 add 2 div xS mul tCy0 tCy1 add 2 div translate tCAng rotate
            tR neg tR neg 3 -1 roll tR 2 mul mul tR 2 mul rectclip tMat setmatrix
            newpath xS 1 scale tGlyph 1 get exec tMat setmatrix fill
        grestore
    } { pop pop } ifelse
} bind def

% -  draws the strike's ink (from tStrikeGlyph: the glyph frame is the CTM)
/tInkStrike {
    tMat currentmatrix pop
    /tGlyph TWOutlines kCode get def
    /sA hA def /sB hB def                       % the strike's prefix
    % density: dryness, pressure (a heavy strike fills the weave in), and
    % the slow ribbon wear across the page (#423: the base is higher and the
    % pressure slope steeper, so the ink thins and a light strike drops out
    % as the references do; a hole's area is only a third of its level)
    % 0.21 at the default /Ribbon 0.7 and mean pressure, 0.36 worn (0.35),
    % 0.8 at /Ribbon 0; -1 per unit of pressure above 1. Below 1 it rises by
    % tLightSlope per unit up to tLightKnee of shortfall and by tLightTail
    % beyond: #477 moved a light strike's loss of ink from holes to thinning
    % (tE, below), so a light strike is faint, not a screen of pin-holes
    1 tRibbon sub dup 0.2 mul exch dup mul dup mul 0.45 mul add 0.15 add
    1 tPr sub dup 0 gt {
        dup tLightKnee gt { tLightKnee sub tLightTail mul tLightKnee tLightSlope mul add } { tLightSlope mul } ifelse
    } { } ifelse add
    wearS 0 gt { tPageWear 0.06 mul wearS 1 2 copy gt { exch } if pop mul add } if
    dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if /pBody exch def
    % a light strike thins: the body is eroded by tELo glyph units per unit
    % of pressure shortfall past tEDead, up to tECap (#477). A photocopy's
    % toner fills the thin strike in again, so a copy is not eroded.
    /tE 1 tPr sub tEDead sub tELo mul dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if dup tECap gt { pop tECap } if def
    tCopyC 0 gt { /tE 0 def } if
    % a photocopy's contrast curve (#214): a dark strike fills in (fewer
    % voids), a light one drops out (more)
    tCopyC 0 gt {
        /pBody pBody 0.18 sub 1 tCopyC 3 mul add mul 0.18 add tCopyC 0.14 mul add
        dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if def
    } if
    % the spread is split: the body carries a fifth of it, the feather
    % reaches to 0.45 of it through a sparse weave (#423; #212 used a third
    % and 0.85)
    /wBodyW inkW 0.2 mul def
    /fW inkW -0.55 mul def
    % the weave's frame: per strike, or per key when strikes do not vary
    wearS 0 eq { tMA tMB kCode mix2 } { sA sB } ifelse
    20 mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def
    /wA hA def /wB hB def
    tMat setmatrix
    advU cellW div dup scale
    1 uni 90 mul rotate
    2 uni tYarn mul 3 uni tYarn mul translate
    tClipM currentmatrix pop
    % the patches that cover the glyph's box, grown by the ink's reach
    tGBox kCode 32 sub get aload pop
    inkW fW add 2 div 4 add /tM exch def
    tM add /tY1 exch def xS mul tM add /tX1 exch def
    tM sub /tY0 exch def xS mul tM sub /tX0 exch def
    /wI0 99999 def /wJ0 99999 def /wI1 -99999 def /wJ1 -99999 def
    [ tX0 tY0 tX1 tY0 tX1 tY1 tX0 tY1 ] aload pop
    4 {
        tMat transform itransform
        tPatch div floor cvi dup wJ0 lt { dup /wJ0 exch def } if
        dup wJ1 gt { /wJ1 exch def } { pop } ifelse
        tPatch div floor cvi dup wI0 lt { dup /wI0 exch def } if
        dup wI1 gt { /wI1 exch def } { pop } ifelse
    } repeat
    tMat setmatrix

    % feather (none at /Ribbon 0: a 0-width stroke is a 1-pixel hairline)
    inkW fW add 0 gt {
        gsave
            pBody 0.5 add tLevel tWeaveClip
            % a thinned strike's fringe shrinks with it (#477)
            tGlyphPath inkW fW add 1 tE tECap div tFringe mul sub mul tPen stroke
        grestore
    } if
    % body; a one-side-heavy key's light side is sparser
    mSS wearM mul dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if /tSide exch def
    tSide 0 gt {
        % the light side thins as well as dots (#477): tE is a variable, not
        % graphics state, so it is put back after
        /tE0 tE def
        /tE tE tSide tESide mul add dup tECap gt { pop tECap } if def
        gsave pBody tSide 0.3 mul add tLevel tWeaveClip tBody grestore
        /tE tE0 def
        gsave
            pBody tLevel tWeaveClip
            tX0 tX1 add 2 div tY0 tY1 add 2 div translate mSA rotate
            0 -2000 2000 4000 rectclip tMat setmatrix
            tBody
        grestore
    } {
        gsave pBody tLevel tWeaveClip tBody grestore
    } ifelse
    % a clogged counter: the dirt packs the counter from one side, over the
    % clog fraction (more on a more worn machine, less on a light strike)
    mClog wearM mul dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if
    tPr dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if mul /tFill exch def
    % a copy fills every counter in over its generations
    tCopyC 0 gt { /tFill tFill tCopyC 0.8 mul add dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if def } if
    tFill 0.05 gt tCBox kCode 32 sub get 2 get 0 gt and {
        % the band runs across the counter's circumscribed circle, from the
        % dirt's side (a fixed direction per key) to the fraction; its edge
        % is ragged: solid to 0.12 short of it, then through a sparse
        % weave to 0.12 past it
        tCBox kCode 32 sub get aload pop
        /tCy1 exch def /tCx1 exch def /tCy0 exch def /tCx0 exch def
        tCx1 tCx0 sub xS mul tCy1 tCy0 sub tLen 2 div /tR exch def
        tMA tMB kCode mix2 /hB exch def /hA exch def 14 uni 360 mul /tCAng exch def
        tFill 0.12 sub pBody tClogBand
        tFill 0.12 add pBody 0.45 add tClogBand
    } if
} bind def

} exec end % TWState

% Load-time state: the defaults, and the frame the default CTM gives, so
% plain findfont/show works without tw-setup.
TWState begin << >> tApply tFrame end

{ /Typewriter 8 dict dup begin
    /FontType 3 def
    /FontMatrix [0.001 0 0 0.001 0 0] def
    /FontBBox [-200 -380 800 940] def
    /Encoding StandardEncoding def
    % The side dict rides in the font: gs makes the font dict read-only at
    % definefont, and the side dict, reached through it, stays writable.
    /TWS TWState def
    /BuildChar {
        exch /TWS get begin
        advU 0 setcharwidth                     % never setcachedevice
        dup 32 gt 1 index 127 lt and { tStrikeGlyph } { pop } ifelse
        end
    } bind def
end definefont pop } exec

%%SelfTest: tw-setup-guards
%   { 5 tw-setup } /tw-options-must-be-a-dict
%   (a number for the options) mustguard
%
%   { << /wear 2 >> tw-setup } /tw-unknown-option
%   (a misspelt key) mustguard
%
%   { << /Preset /portable >> tw-setup } /tw-preset-must-be-manual-electric-or-worn
%   (an unknown preset) mustguard
%
%   { << /Machine 2.5 >> tw-setup } /tw-machine-must-be-an-integer
%   (a real machine number) mustguard
%
%   { << /Seed (1) >> tw-set } /tw-seed-must-be-an-integer
%   (a string seed) mustguard
%
%   { << /Strike 1.0 >> tw-set } /tw-strike-must-be-an-integer
%   (a real strike index) mustguard
%
%   { << /Wear -1 >> tw-setup } /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number
%   (a negative wear) mustguard
%
%   { << /StrikeWear /high >> tw-set } /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number
%   (a name for a layer's wear) mustguard
%
%   { << /Pitch 0 >> tw-setup } /tw-pitch-must-be-a-positive-number
%   (a zero pitch) mustguard
%
%   { << /Size -12 >> tw-setup } /tw-size-must-be-a-positive-number
%   (a negative size) mustguard
%
%   { << /Ink [0 0] >> tw-setup } /tw-ink-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (two components) mustguard
%
%   { << /Ribbon 1.5 >> tw-setup } /tw-ribbon-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a ribbon above 1) mustguard
%
%   { << /InkModel 1 >> tw-set } /tw-inkmodel-must-be-a-boolean
%   (a number for the ink model) mustguard
%
%   { << /Copies -1 >> tw-set } /tw-copies-must-be-a-non-negative-number
%   (a negative number of copies) mustguard
%
%   { << /Machine 9 /Wear -1 >> tw-setup } /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number
%   (one bad key among good ones) mustguard
%   TWState /tMachine get 1 eq (and the good ones were not applied) mustbe
%
%   countdictstack mark { << /Wear -1 >> tw-setup } stopped pop cleartomark countdictstack eq
%   (a rejected tw-setup leaves the dict stack as it was) mustbe
%   countdictstack mark { << /Bogus 1 >> tw-set } stopped pop cleartomark countdictstack eq
%   (a rejected tw-set leaves the dict stack as it was) mustbe
%   countdictstack mark { 7 tw-set } stopped pop cleartomark countdictstack eq
%   (a non-dict leaves the dict stack as it was) mustbe
%
%   { << /Seed -2147483648 /Machine -2147483648 >> tw-set } (the most negative 32-bit seeds) mustpass
%   { << /Seed 2147483647 /Machine 2147483647 >> tw-set } (the largest 32-bit seeds) mustpass
%   { << /Seed 1 /Machine 1 >> tw-set } (back to the defaults) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: tw-line-keys
%   % plain use at another size, on 12pt baselines: every line two
%   % half-line steps below the last, and never a skipped or shared key
%   /Typewriter findfont 12 scalefont setfont
%   /twtOk true def
%   0 1 30 {
%       /twtN exch def
%       0 twtN -12 mul moveto (a) show
%       TWState /kLine get twtN -2 mul ne { /twtOk false def } if
%   } for
%   twtOk (12pt lines at 12pt type step two half-lines each) mustbe
%   /Typewriter findfont 10 scalefont setfont
%   0 -24 moveto (a) show TWState /kLine get -4 eq
%   0 -30 moveto (a) show TWState /kLine get -5 eq and
%   (lines 6pt apart get their own keys) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: tw-setup-applies-presets-and-keys
%   << /Preset /worn >> tw-setup
%   TWState /tWear get 1.5 eq TWState /tRibbon get 0.35 eq and
%   (the worn preset) mustbe
%
%   << /Preset /electric /Ribbon 0.9 >> tw-setup
%   TWState /tWear get 0.7 eq TWState /tRibbon get 0.9 eq and
%   TWState /tFinger get not and
%   (a key given with a preset overrides it) mustbe
%
%   << /Machine 4 >> tw-set
%   TWState /tWear get 0.7 eq TWState /tMachine get 4 eq and
%   (options persist from call to call) mustbe
%
%   << /Pitch 12 /Size 11.5 >> tw-setup
%   currentfont /TWS known (tw-setup sets the font) mustbe
%   (M) stringwidth pop 6 sub abs 0.001 lt (elite: a 6pt advance at 12 cpi) mustbe
%
%   << /Preset /manual /Pitch 10 /Size 12.5 /Machine 1 /Seed 1 >> tw-setup
%   (x) stringwidth pop 7.2 sub abs 0.001 lt (pica: a 7.2pt advance at 10 cpi) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Starts a block of typewriter text: applies the options (a /Preset first, then any other key; options persist from call to call), captures the cell frame (the CTM, moved to the current point if there is one) and sets the /Typewriter font at /Size. Then `show` types. The same /Machine and /Seed always type the same page; the caller's rand stream is untouched.
% @example: (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run (lib/typewriter.ps) run 72 700 moveto << /Machine 7 /Seed 3 >> tw-setup (Dear Sir, I enclose the specimens.) show
% @param: /Preset [name] /manual (a good-condition manual), /electric (a lower-variation manual with even pressure) or /worn (an old, hard-used machine with a dry ribbon); resets /Wear, /Ribbon and the pressure model before the other keys apply (default /manual)
% @param: /Machine [integer] The machine: seeds each key's fixed quirks (offset, bent slug, weight). A new /Seed never re-rolls them (default 1)
% @param: /Seed [integer] The document: seeds the line and strike variation (default 1)
% @param: /Wear [ratio] Multiplies every variation: 0 is a perfect machine, 1 the measured good-condition manual, 1.5 an old one, above 2 for art (default 1)
% @param: /MachineWear [ratio] Multiplies the machine layer only, on top of /Wear (default 1)
% @param: /LineWear [ratio] Multiplies the line layer only, on top of /Wear (default 1)
% @param: /StrikeWear [ratio] Multiplies the strike layer only, pressure included, on top of /Wear (default 1)
% @param: /Pitch [count per inch] Characters per inch: 10 is pica, 12 elite. Sets the advance; the glyphs are squeezed to fit it (default 10)
% @param: /Size [points] The type's em, which sets its height (default 12.5; 11.5 suits elite)
% @param: /Ink [rgb array] The ribbon's colour; a worn ribbon is a lighter, browner colour here (default [0.13 0.13 0.15])
% @param: /Ribbon [0..1] Ribbon wetness: how far the ink spreads past the type face (0 for none), and how solid the ink is: a drier ribbon leaves more of the weave's voids open (default 0.7)
% @param: /InkModel [boolean] true draws ribbon ink (the weave's voids, a feathered edge, clogged counters, a heavy side, density from pressure and /Ribbon); false draws flat ink, the glyph filled and stroked solid (default true)
% @param: /Strike [integer] The strike index: a second strike at the same cell (a strikeover, a bold double strike) needs a new index to get its own variation (default 0)
% @param: /Copies [count] Photocopy generations the type has been through, 0 for the original: each adds spread, fills the counters in and sharpens the contrast of the ink's coverage, so dark strikes close up and light ones drop out; 1 is a first copy, 3 a copy of a copy of a copy (default 0)
/tw-setup {
    TWState begin tApply tFrame end
    /Typewriter findfont TWState /tSize get scalefont setfont
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Changes /Typewriter options inside a block without moving its cell frame: a red ribbon run (/Ink), the strike index for an overtype (/Strike), a heavier hand. Takes tw-setup's keys; a /Size given also resets the font.
% @example: (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run (lib/typewriter.ps) run 72 700 moveto << >> tw-setup (word) show 72 700 moveto << /Strike 1 >> tw-set (____) show << /Strike 0 >> tw-set
% @param: /Preset [name] As tw-setup: /manual, /electric or /worn (no default: the current options stay)
% @param: /Machine [integer] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Seed [integer] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Wear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /MachineWear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /LineWear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /StrikeWear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Pitch [count per inch] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Size [points] As tw-setup; also resets the font at the new size (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Ink [rgb array] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Ribbon [0..1] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /InkModel [boolean] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Strike [integer] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Copies [count] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
/tw-set {
    dup type /dicttype eq { dup /Size known } { false } ifelse exch
    TWState begin tApply end
    { /Typewriter findfont TWState /tSize get scalefont setfont } if
} bind def

% ---- layout: tw-type, tw-linecount, tw-pageheight (#213) ------------------
% One layout makes a list of line records [a b col0] (cells a..b-1 of the
% parsed text, the first at column col0). tw-linecount returns the length of
% that list and tw-type draws from it, so the two always agree. Everything
% private here lives in TWState under the prefix ly (artkit owns tw*).
%
% The text is parsed once into three parallel strings, one byte per cell:
%   lyC  the character typed
%   lyA  attribute bits: 1 underline, 2 glued (inside <o>..</o>: one
%        unbreakable token), 4 redacted
%   lyO  the character struck over it (0 for none)
% Markup, with no escapes needed (any other < is literal):
%   <u>text</u>      underlined: a second pass of underscores
%   <x>text</x>      crossed out: x struck over each character
%   <o>old|new</o>   overtyped: new struck over old; the token is as wide as
%                    the longer side and never breaks across a line
%   <r>text</r>      redacted (#214): typed, then covered with a bar over
%                    each run on a line, by the /Redactor procedure (a
%                    plain black bar when there is none)
% Bytes above 126 are not on the machine: each takes one blank cell, and
% tw-linecount counts them the same way. Control characters other than tab
% and newline are blank cells; carriage returns are dropped.
TWState begin {

% a n ch ... helpers
/lyRep {                                    % n ch -> string of n ch
    /lrC exch def /lrN exch def
    lrN string /lrS exch def
    0 1 lrN 1 sub { lrS exch lrC put } for
    lrS
} bind def
/lyCat {                                    % a b -> ab
    /lcB exch def /lcA exch def
    lcA length lcB length add string
    dup 0 lcA putinterval
    dup lcA length lcB putinterval
} bind def

% ---- option checks (layout keys; the typewriter keys are tw-setup's) -------
/lyIsStr { type /stringtype eq } bind def
/lyIsSpacing { dup tIsNum { dup 0.5 ge exch 20 le and } { pop false } ifelse } bind def
/lyIsCount { dup tIsInt { 0 ge } { pop false } ifelse } bind def
/lyIsTabs {
    dup type /integertype eq { 0 gt } {
        dup type /arraytype eq 1 index xcheck not and {
            /ltOk true def /ltPrev -1 def
            {
                dup type /integertype eq {
                    dup ltPrev gt { /ltPrev exch def } { pop /ltOk false def } ifelse
                } { pop /ltOk false def } ifelse
            } forall
            ltOk
        } { pop false } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyIsPN {
    dup type /nametype eq {
        dup /none eq 1 index /top eq or exch /bottom eq or
    } { pop false } ifelse
} bind def
/lyIsProc { dup type /arraytype eq { xcheck } { pop false } ifelse } bind def
/lyMemoKeys <<
    /To /lyIsStr  /From /lyIsStr  /Cc /lyIsStr  /Subject /lyIsStr
    /Date /lyIsStr  /Title /lyIsStr  /Rule /tIsBool
>> def
/lyIsMemo {
    dup type /dicttype eq {
        true exch {
            /lmV exch def /lmK exch def
            lyMemoKeys lmK known { lmV lyMemoKeys lmK get cvx exec and } { pop false } ifelse
        } forall
    } { pop false } ifelse
} bind def

% key -> [ internal-name check guard ]
/lyKeys <<
    /Text        [ /lyText /lyIsStr     /tw-text-must-be-a-string ]
    /Width       [ /lyW    /tIsPos      /tw-width-must-be-a-positive-number ]
    /Height      [ /lyH    /tIsPos      /tw-height-must-be-a-positive-number ]
    /Margin      [ /lyM    /tIsNonNeg   /tw-margin-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /MarginLeft  [ /lyML   /tIsNonNeg   /tw-margin-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /MarginRight [ /lyMR   /tIsNonNeg   /tw-margin-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /MarginTop   [ /lyMT   /tIsNonNeg   /tw-margin-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /MarginBottom [ /lyMB  /tIsNonNeg   /tw-margin-must-be-a-non-negative-number ]
    /Spacing     [ /lySp   /lyIsSpacing /tw-spacing-must-be-0.5-to-20 ]
    /Indent      [ /lyInd  /lyIsCount   /tw-indent-must-be-a-non-negative-integer ]
    /Tabs        [ /lyTabs /lyIsTabs    /tw-tabs-must-be-a-positive-integer-or-ascending-columns ]
    /PageNumbers [ /lyPN   /lyIsPN      /tw-pagenumbers-must-be-none-top-or-bottom ]
    /PageStart   [ /lyPS   /lyIsProc    /tw-pagestart-must-be-a-procedure ]
    /Memo        [ /lyMemo /lyIsMemo    /tw-memo-must-be-a-dict-of-known-string-keys ]
    /Redactor    [ /lyRd   /lyIsProc    /tw-redactor-must-be-a-procedure ]
>> def
/lyDefaults {
    /lyText () def  /lyW 612 def  /lyH 792 def  /lyM 72 def
    /lyML -1 def  /lyMR -1 def  /lyMT -1 def  /lyMB -1 def
    /lySp 1 def  /lyInd 0 def  /lyTabs 5 def  /lyPN /none def
    /lyPS null def  /lyMemo null def  /lyRd null def
} bind def

% opts -> typeOpts. Reads the layout keys into ly variables and returns a
% dict of the rest, for tw-setup. A key in neither set is an error, so a
% misspelling never passes silently. TWState is current: each guard ends it
% first, as tApply's do.
/lySplit {
    dup type /dicttype ne { end tw-options-must-be-a-dict } if
    lyDefaults
    dup length dict /lyRest exch def
    {                                       % key value
        1 index lyKeys exch known {
            lyKeys 2 index get aload pop
            /lyG exch def /lyCk exch def /lyVar exch def
            % the value stays on the operand stack: a procedure (/PageStart)
            % would run if it were fetched by name
            dup lyCk cvx exec { lyVar exch def pop } { lyG end cvx exec } ifelse
        } {
            tKeys 2 index known not { end tw-unknown-option } if
            lyRest 3 1 roll put
        } ifelse
    } forall
    lyRest
} bind def

% typeOpts -> -  : the pitch and size this call types at, without applying
% the options (tw-linecount and tw-pageheight must not change the machine)
/lyEff {
    dup /Pitch known {
        dup /Pitch get dup tIsPos not { end tw-pitch-must-be-a-positive-number } if
        /lyP exch def
    } { /lyP tPitch def } ifelse
    dup /Size known {
        dup /Size get dup tIsPos not { end tw-size-must-be-a-positive-number } if
        /lySz exch def
    } { /lySz tSize def } ifelse
    pop
} bind def

% ---- geometry --------------------------------------------------------------
/lyGeomX {
    /lyPt 72 lyP div def                    % the advance, points
    /lyL lyML 0 lt { lyM } { lyML } ifelse def
    /lyR lyMR 0 lt { lyM } { lyMR } ifelse def
    /lyTop lyMT 0 lt { lyM } { lyMT } ifelse def
    /lyBot lyMB 0 lt { lyM } { lyMB } ifelse def
    /lyCols lyW lyL sub lyR sub lyPt div 0.0001 add floor cvi dup 1 lt { pop 1 } if def
    % single spacing is 12pt; the platen steps in 6pt half-lines
    /lyStep lySp 2 mul round cvi 6 mul def
    /lyHang 10 def
} bind def
/lyGeomY {
    /lyY0 lyH lyTop sub 0.75 lySz mul sub def        % the first baseline
    /lyNLP lyY0 lyBot sub 0.001 add lyStep div floor cvi 1 add dup 1 lt { pop 1 } if def
} bind def

% string -> a copy with newlines and carriage returns as spaces: a memo field
% is one paragraph, so the hang bookkeeping stays in step with the text
/lyOneLine {
    dup length string copy /lo exch def
    0 1 lo length 1 sub {
        /loI exch def
        lo loI get dup 10 eq exch 13 eq or { lo loI 32 put } if
    } for
    lo
} bind def

% ---- the memo header -------------------------------------------------------
% sets lyHg0 lyHg1 (the paragraphs hung at lyHang) and lyBody0 (the first
% paragraph of the body); returns the header's text
/lyMemoText {
    () /lyAcc exch def
    /lyPn 0 def
    lyMemo /Title known { lyMemo /Title get } { (MEMORANDUM) } ifelse /lyTt exch def
    lyTt length 0 gt {
        lyAcc
        lyCols lyTt length sub 2 idiv dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if 32 lyRep lyCat
        (<u>) lyCat lyTt lyCat (</u>) lyCat (\n\n) lyCat /lyAcc exch def
        /lyPn lyPn 2 add def
    } if
    /lyHg0 lyPn def
    [ [/To (TO:)] [/From (FROM:)] [/Cc (CC:)] [/Subject (SUBJECT:)] [/Date (DATE:)] ] {
        aload pop /lyLab exch def /lyKey exch def
        lyMemo lyKey known {
            lyAcc lyLab lyCat
            lyHang lyLab length sub 32 lyRep lyCat
            lyMemo lyKey get lyOneLine lyCat (\n) lyCat /lyAcc exch def
            /lyPn lyPn 1 add def
        } if
    } forall
    /lyHg1 lyPn def
    lyPn 0 gt {
        lyMemo /Rule known { lyMemo /Rule get } { true } ifelse {
            lyAcc lyCols 95 lyRep lyCat (\n) lyCat /lyAcc exch def
            /lyPn lyPn 1 add def
        } if
        lyAcc (\n) lyCat /lyAcc exch def
        /lyPn lyPn 1 add def
    } if
    /lyBody0 lyPn def
    lyAcc
} bind def
% -> the whole text: the memo header (if any) and /Text
/lyDoc {
    /lyHg0 -1 def  /lyHg1 -1 def  /lyBody0 0 def
    lyMemo null eq { lyText } { lyMemoText lyText lyCat } ifelse
} bind def

% ---- parsing ---------------------------------------------------------------
/lyHas {                                    % str -> bool : str is at lyI
    /lhS exch def
    lhS length lyI add lyTn le { lyT lyI lhS length getinterval lhS eq } { false } ifelse
} bind def
/lyTagTab [
    [ (<u>)  { /lyIu lyIu 1 add def } bind ]
    [ (</u>) { /lyIu lyIu 1 sub dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if def } bind ]
    [ (<x>)  { /lyIx lyIx 1 add def } bind ]
    [ (</x>) { /lyIx lyIx 1 sub dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if def } bind ]
    [ (<o>)  { lyIo 0 eq { /lyIo 1 def /lyOs lyN def } if } bind ]
    [ (</o>) { /lyIo 0 def } bind ]
    [ (<r>)  { /lyIr lyIr 1 add def } bind ]
    [ (</r>) { /lyIr lyIr 1 sub dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if def } bind ]
] def
/lyTag {                                    % -> bool : consumed a tag at lyI
    /lyTr false def
    lyTagTab {
        dup 0 get lyHas {
            dup 1 get exec
            0 get length lyI add /lyI exch def
            /lyTr true def exit
        } { pop } ifelse
    } forall
    lyTr
} bind def
/lyAdd {                                    % c -> -  : append a cell
    /lyCh exch def
    lyCh 13 eq { } {
        lyCh 10 eq { /lyIo 0 def } if
        lyCh 32 lt lyCh 9 ne and lyCh 10 ne and { /lyCh 32 def } if
        lyIo 0 gt lyCh 9 eq and { /lyCh 32 def } if
        lyIu 0 gt { 1 } { 0 } ifelse lyIo 0 gt { 2 } { 0 } ifelse or
        lyIr 0 gt { 4 } { 0 } ifelse or /lyAt exch def
        lyIo 2 eq {
            lyCh 32 gt { lyCh } { 0 } ifelse /lyOv exch def
            lyOv 0 gt { /lyAnyO true def } if
            lyAt 4 and 0 ne { /lyAnyR true def } if
            lyOp lyN lyOs sub lt {
                lyO lyOs lyOp add lyOv put
                lyAt 4 and 0 ne { lyA lyOs lyOp add 2 copy get 4 or put } if
            } {
                lyC lyN 32 put  lyA lyN lyAt put  lyO lyN lyOv put
                /lyN lyN 1 add def
            } ifelse
            /lyOp lyOp 1 add def
        } {
            lyIo 1 eq lyCh 124 eq and {
                /lyIo 2 def  /lyOp 0 def
            } {
                lyC lyN lyCh put  lyA lyN lyAt put
                lyIx 0 gt lyCh 32 gt and { 120 /lyAnyO true def } { 0 } ifelse
                lyO lyN 3 -1 roll put
                lyAt 1 and 0 ne { /lyAnyU true def } if
                lyAt 4 and 0 ne { /lyAnyR true def } if
                /lyN lyN 1 add def
            } ifelse
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyParse {                                  % string -> -
    /lyT exch def  /lyTn lyT length def
    /lyC lyTn string def  /lyA lyTn string def  /lyO lyTn string def
    /lyN 0 def  /lyIu 0 def  /lyIx 0 def  /lyIo 0 def  /lyIr 0 def  /lyI 0 def
    /lyAnyO false def  /lyAnyU false def  /lyAnyR false def
    {
        lyI lyTn ge { exit } if
        lyTag not { lyT lyI get lyAdd /lyI lyI 1 add def } if
    } loop
} bind def

% ---- wrapping ---------------------------------------------------------------
/lyTab {                                    % col -> the next tab stop
    lyTabs type /integertype eq {
        lyTabs idiv 1 add lyTabs mul
    } {
        /ltC exch def  /ltR ltC 1 add def
        lyTabs { dup ltC gt { /ltR exch def exit } { pop } ifelse } forall
        ltR
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyNext { 9 eq { lyTab } { 1 add } ifelse } bind def        % col c -> col'
/lyIsWord {                                 % i -> bool
    /lwI exch def
    lyC lwI get 32 gt lyA lwI get 2 and 0 ne or
} bind def
/lyEmit {                                   % a b col0 -> -
    /leC exch def /leB exch def /leA exch def
    lyLines lyNL 3 mul leA put
    lyLines lyNL 3 mul 1 add leB put
    lyLines lyNL 3 mul 2 add leC put
    /lyNL lyNL 1 add def
} bind def
% start end index -> -  : wrap one paragraph greedily by word. Whitespace
% where a line breaks is dropped, a word wider than a line is split, and
% leading whitespace on a paragraph's first line is kept.
/lyPara {
    /paI exch def /paE exch def /paS exch def
    /paFi paI lyBody0 lt { 0 } { lyInd } ifelse def
    /paCi paI lyHg0 ge paI lyHg1 lt and { lyHang } { 0 } ifelse def
    paFi lyCols 1 sub gt { /paFi lyCols 1 sub def } if
    paCi lyCols 1 sub gt { /paCi lyCols 1 sub def } if
    paS paE eq { paS paS paFi lyEmit } {
        /paPos paS def  /paCol paFi def  /paA paS def  /paC0 paFi def
        /paNB false def  /paWs -1 def
        {
            paPos paE ge { exit } if
            paPos lyIsWord not {
                paWs 0 lt { /paWs paPos def } if
                /paCol paCol lyC paPos get lyNext def
                /paPos paPos 1 add def
            } {
                /paWe paPos def
                { paWe paE ge { exit } if paWe lyIsWord not { exit } if /paWe paWe 1 add def } loop
                paNB paCol paWe paPos sub add lyCols gt and {
                    paA paWs 0 ge { paWs } { paPos } ifelse paC0 lyEmit
                    /paA paPos def  /paCol paCi def  /paC0 paCi def  /paNB false def
                } if
                {
                    paPos paWe ge { exit } if
                    paCol lyCols ge {
                        paA paPos paC0 lyEmit
                        /paA paPos def  /paCol paCi def  /paC0 paCi def
                    } if
                    /paCol paCol 1 add def  /paPos paPos 1 add def  /paNB true def
                } loop
                /paWs -1 def
            } ifelse
        } loop
        paA paE paC0 lyEmit
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyLayout {
    /lyNL 0 def
    /lyLines lyN 2 mul 4 add 3 mul array def
    lyN 0 gt {
        /lyPs 0 def  /lyPi 0 def
        {
            /lyPe lyPs def
            { lyPe lyN ge { exit } if lyC lyPe get 10 eq { exit } if /lyPe lyPe 1 add def } loop
            lyPs lyPe lyPi lyPara
            lyPe 1 add dup lyN ge { pop exit } if /lyPs exch def
            /lyPi lyPi 1 add def
        } loop
    } if
} bind def

% ---- drawing ----------------------------------------------------------------
/lyIncl {                                   % cell pass -> bool : is it struck in this pass
    /liP exch def /liJ exch def
    liP 0 eq { lyC liJ get 32 gt } {
        liP 1 eq { lyO liJ get 32 gt } {
            lyA liJ get 1 and 0 ne lyC liJ get 9 ne and
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyShowRun {                                % start end col pass -> -
    /lrP exch def /lrCol exch def /lrE exch def /lrS exch def
    lyL lrCol lyPt mul add lyYr moveto
    lrP 0 eq { lyC lrS lrE lrS sub getinterval show } {
        lrP 1 eq { lyO lrS lrE lrS sub getinterval show } {
            lrE lrS sub 95 lyRep show
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyLinePass {                               % pass -> -  (the line is lyLa lyLb lyLc)
    /lpP exch def
    /lpCol lyLc def  /lpIn false def
    lyLa 1 lyLb 1 sub {
        /lpJ exch def
        lpJ lpP lyIncl {
            lpIn not { /lpIn true def /lpS lpJ def /lpC0 lpCol def } if
        } {
            lpIn { lpS lpJ lpC0 lpP lyShowRun /lpIn false def } if
        } ifelse
        /lpCol lpCol lyC lpJ get lyNext def
    } for
    lpIn { lpS lyLb lpC0 lpP lyShowRun } if
} bind def
% the line's redacted runs: each is handed, as x0 x1 baseline size, to the
% /Redactor procedure with TWState ended (as /PageStart is), else covered by a
% plain black bar. The cells keep their column, so a run is a whole number of
% cells wide.
/lyRedactRun {                              % c0 c1 -> -
    lyPt mul lyL add exch lyPt mul lyL add exch
    lyYr tSize
    /lrdS exch def /lrdY exch def /lrdX1 exch def /lrdX0 exch def
    /lyRd load null ne {
        lrdX0 lrdX1 lrdY lrdS
        gsave /lyRd load end exec TWState begin grestore
    } {
        gsave 0 setgray newpath
        lrdX0 lrdY lrdS 0.25 mul sub moveto lrdX1 lrdY lrdS 0.25 mul sub lineto
        lrdX1 lrdY lrdS 0.8 mul add lineto lrdX0 lrdY lrdS 0.8 mul add lineto
        closepath fill grestore
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyRedactLine {
    /lrCol lyLc def  /lrIn false def
    lyLa 1 lyLb 1 sub {
        /lrJ exch def
        lyA lrJ get 4 and 0 ne {
            lrIn not { /lrIn true def /lrC0 lrCol def } if
        } {
            lrIn { lrC0 lrCol lyRedactRun /lrIn false def } if
        } ifelse
        /lrCol lrCol lyC lrJ get lyNext def
    } for
    lrIn { lrC0 lrCol lyRedactRun } if
} bind def
/lyDrawLine {                               % global row -> -
    /ldR exch def
    lyLines ldR 3 mul get /lyLa exch def
    lyLines ldR 3 mul 1 add get /lyLb exch def
    lyLines ldR 3 mul 2 add get /lyLc exch def
    /lyYr lyY0 ldR lyR0 sub lyStep mul sub def
    /tStrike lyS0 def
    0 lyLinePass
    lyAnyO { /tStrike lyS0 1 add def 1 lyLinePass } if
    lyAnyU { /tStrike lyS0 2 add def 2 lyLinePass } if
    /tStrike lyS0 def
    lyAnyR { lyRedactLine } if
} bind def
/lyPageNum {
    lyPN /none eq { } {
        lyPN /bottom eq {
            (- ) lyPg 1 add 12 string cvs lyCat ( -) lyCat /lnS exch def
            lyCols lnS length sub 2 idiv
            lyY0 lyBot 2 div sub lyStep div round cvi dup lyNLP lt { pop lyNLP } if
            true
        } {
            lyPg 0 gt {
                lyPg 1 add 12 string cvs /lnS exch def
                lyCols lnS length sub
                lyY0 lyH lyTop 2 div sub sub lyStep div round cvi dup -1 gt { pop -1 } if
                true
            } { false } ifelse
        } ifelse
        {
            /lnRow exch def /lnCol exch def
            /tStrike lyS0 def
            lyL lnCol 0 gt { lnCol } { 0 } ifelse lyPt mul add
            lyY0 lnRow lyStep mul sub moveto
            lnS show
        } if
    } ifelse
} bind def
/lyPage {                                   % page index -> -
    /lyPg exch def
    lyPg 0 gt { showpage } if
    /lyPS load null ne { gsave /lyPS load end exec TWState begin grestore } if
    newpath lyL lyY0 lyPg lyNLP mul lyStep mul add moveto tFrame
    /Typewriter findfont tSize scalefont setfont
    /lyR0 lyPg lyNLP mul def
    lyNL lyR0 sub dup lyNLP gt { pop lyNLP } if /lyCnt exch def
    0 1 lyCnt 1 sub { lyR0 add lyDrawLine } for
    lyPageNum
} bind def
/lyDraw {
    /lyS0 tStrike def
    /lyPages lyNL lyNLP add 1 sub lyNLP idiv dup 1 lt { pop 1 } if def
    0 1 lyPages 1 sub { lyPage } for
    /tStrike lyS0 def
    % leave the carriage at the start of the next line
    lyL lyY0 lyPages 1 sub lyNLP mul lyStep mul add lyNL lyStep mul sub moveto
} bind def

} exec end % TWState (layout)

%%SelfTest: tw-type-guards
%   { 5 tw-type } /tw-options-must-be-a-dict
%   (a number for the options) mustguard
%
%   { << /Txt (a) >> tw-type } /tw-unknown-option
%   (a misspelt key) mustguard
%
%   { << /Text 5 >> tw-linecount } /tw-text-must-be-a-string
%   (a number for the text) mustguard
%
%   { << /Spacing 0 >> tw-type } /tw-spacing-must-be-0.5-to-20
%   (spacing below half a line) mustguard
%
%   { << /Margin -1 >> tw-pageheight } /tw-margin-must-be-a-non-negative-number
%   (a negative margin) mustguard
%
%   { << /Tabs [ 8 4 ] >> tw-linecount } /tw-tabs-must-be-a-positive-integer-or-ascending-columns
%   (tab stops out of order) mustguard
%
%   { << /Tabs { 6 20 } >> tw-linecount } /tw-tabs-must-be-a-positive-integer-or-ascending-columns
%   (a procedure for the tab stops) mustguard
%
%   { << /Tabs 0 >> tw-linecount } /tw-tabs-must-be-a-positive-integer-or-ascending-columns
%   (a tab stop every 0 columns) mustguard
%
%   { << /PageNumbers /middle >> tw-type } /tw-pagenumbers-must-be-none-top-or-bottom
%   (an unknown page number place) mustguard
%
%   { << /PageStart 5 >> tw-type } /tw-pagestart-must-be-a-procedure
%   (a number for PageStart) mustguard
%
%   { << /Redactor 5 >> tw-type } /tw-redactor-must-be-a-procedure
%   (a number for the redactor) mustguard
%
%   { << /Memo << /Sbject (x) >> >> tw-linecount } /tw-memo-must-be-a-dict-of-known-string-keys
%   (a misspelt memo key) mustguard
%
%   { << /Pitch 0 >> tw-linecount } /tw-pitch-must-be-a-positive-number
%   (a zero pitch counts nothing) mustguard
%
%   { << /Wear -1 /Text (a) >> tw-type } /tw-wear-must-be-a-non-negative-number
%   (tw-setup's own check reaches tw-type) mustguard
%
%   countdictstack mark { << /Spacing 0 >> tw-type } stopped pop cleartomark countdictstack eq
%   (a rejected tw-type leaves the dict stack as it was) mustbe
%   countdictstack mark { << /Wear -1 /Text (a) >> tw-type } stopped pop cleartomark countdictstack eq
%   (tw-setup rejecting a key leaves the dict stack as it was) mustbe
%   countdictstack mark { << /Bogus 1 >> tw-linecount } stopped pop cleartomark countdictstack eq
%   (a rejected tw-linecount leaves the dict stack as it was) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: tw-linecount-wraps
%   << /Text (aaa bbb ccc ddd eee fff) /Width 144 /Margin 36 >> tw-linecount 3 eq
%   (72pt of text at pica is 10 columns: two 3-letter words per line) mustbe
%   << /Text (a\nb\n\nc\n) /Width 612 >> tw-linecount 4 eq
%   (newlines break, a blank line stays, a final newline ends the last line) mustbe
%   << /Text () >> tw-linecount 0 eq
%   (no text, no lines) mustbe
%   << /Text (aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa) /Width 252 /Margin 36 >> tw-linecount 3 eq
%   (a word wider than a line is split, 70 letters in 30 columns) mustbe
%   << /Text (<o>ab|c</o> <u>d</u><x>e</x>) /Width 612 >> tw-linecount 1 eq
%   (markup takes no cells) mustbe
%   << /Text (a <r>b c</r> d\n<r>e</r>) >> tw-linecount 2 eq
%   (redaction tags take no cells) mustbe
%   << /Text (a b) /Memo << /To (x) >> >> tw-linecount 6 eq
%   (a memo: title, blank, one field, rule, blank, then the text) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Types a text on the page the way a typist would: word-wrapped to the margins, with tab stops, single/1.5/double spacing (the platen advances in half-lines, and the font's line layer gives each line its own carriage-return offset, advance error and baseline drift), underlining and strikeovers by markup, an optional memo header (TO/FROM/SUBJECT/DATE) and page numbers. Takes one options dict: the layout keys below plus any tw-setup key (/Machine /Seed /Wear /Pitch /Size /Ink /Ribbon /Preset ...), which persist as they do for tw-setup. Text that overflows a page is continued on the next one after a showpage; the last page is left for the caller to showpage. Markup in /Text, with no escapes needed: <u>underlined</u>, <x>crossed out</x> (x struck over each letter), <o>old|new</o> (new overtyped on old, one unbreakable token as wide as the longer side), <r>redacted</r> (typed, then covered by a bar over the run on each line, by /Redactor or a plain black bar; a literal <r> in a text is markup). A tab goes to the next tab stop, bytes above 126 are blank cells, and a blank line is kept. tw-linecount always agrees with it on line breaks. Leaves the carriage at the start of the line after the last one typed. The text is one PostScript string, so Ghostscript's 65,535-byte string limit caps it (about 30 pages).
% @example: (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run (lib/typewriter.ps) run << /Text (Dear Sir,\n\nI enclose the <u>specimens</u> you asked for, and a <o>wrong|right</o> correction.) /Machine 7 /Seed 3 >> tw-type showpage
% @param: /Text [string] The text to type, with the <u> <x> <o> <r> markup; newlines break lines, a blank line stays blank (default empty)
% @param: /Width [points] Page width (default 612)
% @param: /Height [points] Page height; it sets how many lines fit before the next page, tw-pageheight gives the height that holds all the text (default 792)
% @param: /Margin [points] All four margins (default 72)
% @param: /MarginLeft [points] The left margin, if not /Margin (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginRight [points] The right margin, if not /Margin (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginTop [points] The top margin, if not /Margin (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginBottom [points] The bottom margin, if not /Margin (default /Margin)
% @param: /Spacing [multiple of single spacing] 1 is single (12pt), 1.5 and 2 double; rounded to the platen's half-line steps, 0.5 to 20 (default 1)
% @param: /Indent [count] Columns to indent the first line of each paragraph (default 0)
% @param: /Tabs [count or array of count] A tab goes to the next stop: a number is a stop every N columns, an array lists ascending stop columns counted from the left margin (default 5)
% @param: /PageNumbers [name] /none, /bottom (centred "- 2 -" on every page) or /top (right-aligned "2" from page 2) (default /none)
% @param: /PageStart [proc] Run before each page is typed, the first included, inside a gsave: paint the paper here. Pages after the first follow a showpage, so they are typed in the default graphics state, not a rotated or scaled one (default none)
% @param: /Memo [dict] A memo header typed above the text: /To /From /Cc /Subject /Date /Title (strings; /Title defaults to MEMORANDUM and is centred and underlined) and /Rule (boolean, the underscore line under the header, default true); a long field wraps hung under its text (default none)
% @param: /Redactor [proc] Covers each run of <r>..</r> text on a line: called as x0 x1 baseline size [points], in the page frame inside a gsave, with the typewriter's side dictionary ended (lib/released.ps rel-redact is one); without it a plain black bar is drawn (default none)
% @param: /Preset [name] As tw-setup: /manual, /electric or /worn (no default: the current options stay)
% @param: /Machine [integer] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Seed [integer] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Strike [integer] As tw-setup; the text is struck at this index, strikeovers at +1 and underlining at +2 (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Wear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /MachineWear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /LineWear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /StrikeWear [ratio] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Pitch [count per inch] As tw-setup: 10 is pica, 12 elite; it sets the columns per line (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Size [points] As tw-setup; it also sets the first baseline, 0.75 em below the top margin (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Ink [rgb array] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Ribbon [0..1] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /InkModel [boolean] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Copies [count] As tw-setup (no default: the current value stays)
/tw-type {
    TWState begin
    lySplit                                 % the layout keys read; the rest left
    end tw-setup TWState begin              % applied (and checked) by tw-setup
    /lyP tPitch def  /lySz tSize def
    lyGeomX lyGeomY lyDoc lyParse lyLayout
    lyDraw
    end
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Returns the number of lines tw-type would type for the same options, drawing nothing and changing no typewriter option: for auto-sizing a page. It counts typed lines (blank lines and a memo header included, page numbers not) over all pages, and always agrees with tw-type on the line breaks. Only /Pitch among the typewriter keys matters to it.
% @example: (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run (lib/typewriter.ps) run << /Text (a short note) /Width 288 >> tw-linecount =
% @param: /Text [string] As tw-type (default empty)
% @param: /Width [points] As tw-type (default 612)
% @param: /Margin [points] As tw-type (default 72)
% @param: /MarginLeft [points] As tw-type (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginRight [points] As tw-type (default /Margin)
% @param: /Indent [count] As tw-type (default 0)
% @param: /Tabs [count or array of count] As tw-type (default 5)
% @param: /Memo [dict] As tw-type (default none)
% @param: /Pitch [count per inch] As tw-type: it sets the columns per line (no default: the current value stays)
/tw-linecount {
    TWState begin
    lySplit lyEff
    lyGeomX lyDoc lyParse lyLayout
    lyNL
    end
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Returns the page height, in points, that holds all the text tw-type would type for the same options on one page: the top margin, the first line's 0.75 em, the lines at /Spacing and the bottom margin. Use it to size a page to its text, as the headless pre-pass of scripts/typewrite.sh does with --height fit. /Height is ignored.
% @example: (lib/typewriter_outlines.ps) run (lib/typewriter.ps) run << /Text (a short note) /Width 288 >> tw-pageheight =
% @param: /Text [string] As tw-type (default empty)
% @param: /Width [points] As tw-type (default 612)
% @param: /Margin [points] As tw-type (default 72)
% @param: /MarginLeft [points] As tw-type (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginRight [points] As tw-type (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginTop [points] As tw-type (default /Margin)
% @param: /MarginBottom [points] As tw-type (default /Margin)
% @param: /Spacing [multiple of single spacing] As tw-type (default 1)
% @param: /Indent [count] As tw-type (default 0)
% @param: /Tabs [count or array of count] As tw-type (default 5)
% @param: /Memo [dict] As tw-type (default none)
% @param: /Pitch [count per inch] As tw-type (no default: the current value stays)
% @param: /Size [points] As tw-type (no default: the current value stays)
/tw-pageheight {
    TWState begin
    lySplit lyEff
    lyGeomX lyDoc lyParse lyLayout
    lyTop 0.75 lySz mul add
    lyNL 1 sub dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if lyStep mul add
    lyBot add ceiling cvi
    end
} bind def
